## Why This Is Asked
Designing an incident response plan with region-aware routing and safe rollback demonstrates depth in observability design and production reliability.

## Key Concepts
- Observability and signal fusion
- Region-based SLOs and traffic shaping
- Circuit breakers and fallback paths
- Canary and synthetic testing at the edge

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo per-region circuit breaker sketch
const breakers = new Map();
function allowed(region){
  const b = breakers.get(region) ?? {open:false, since:0, failures:0};
  return !b.open;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle compliance and data sovereignty in this plan?
- How would you validate rollback safety under real traffic with limited data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to respond to production incidents with a concrete, beginner-friendly plan focusing on observable signals, containment, rollback, and validation under time pressure.

## Key Concepts
- Observability: latency p95, error rate, queue depth, redis/backlog metrics
- Containment: throttle concurrency, pause new work, revert patch safely
- Rollback & Validation: restore previous config, synthetic tests, end-to-end checks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple end-to-end validation stub
async function checkDelivery(queueClient, producer) {
  const msg = {id: 'test', payload: 'ping'};
  await producer.send(msg);
  const ok = await queueClient.awaitDeliveryAck(msg.id, 5000);
  return ok;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate the rollback trigger and rollback-on-fail thresholds?
- Which metrics would you add to distinguish transient spikes from persistent issues?

## Why This Is Asked
